window.docContents[28].resolve({"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Logical-Equivalence/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Logical-Equivalence--Examples":{"contents":"Each equivalence below is verified by a truth table. Two propositions are equivalent when their final columns agree in every row, and a tautology has a column that is true in every row.\n\nExample 1. Double negation returns the original proposition.\n\n* P* ¬P* ¬¬P\n* T* F* T\n* F* T* F\n\nExample 2. The excluded middle P ∨ ¬P is a tautology.\n\n* P* ¬P* P ∨ ¬P\n* T* F* T\n* F* T* T\n\nExample 3. Non-contradiction ¬(P ∧ ¬P) is a tautology.\n\n* P* ¬P* P ∧ ¬P* ¬(P ∧ ¬P)\n* T* F* F* T\n* F* T* F* T\n\nExample 4. The first De Morgan law.\n\n* P* Q* P ∧ Q* ¬(P ∧ Q)* ¬P* ¬Q* ¬P ∨ ¬Q\n* T* T* T* F* F* F* F\n* T* F* F* T* F* T* T\n* F* T* F* T* T* F* T\n* F* F* F* T* T* T* T\n\nExample 5. Disjunction commutes.\n\n* P* Q* P ∨ Q* Q ∨ P\n* T* T* T* T\n* T* F* T* T\n* F* T* T* T\n* F* F* F* F\n\nExample 6. Disjunction is idempotent.\n\n* P* P ∨ P\n* T* T\n* F* F\n\nExample 7. The contrapositive.\n\n* P* Q* P → Q* ¬Q* ¬P* ¬Q → ¬P\n* T* T* T* F* F* T\n* T* F* F* T* F* F\n* F* T* T* F* T* T\n* F* F* T* T* T* T\n\nExample 8. Material implication.\n\n* P* Q* P → Q* ¬P* ¬P ∨ Q\n* T* T* T* F* T\n* T* F* F* F* F\n* F* T* T* T* T\n* F* F* T* T* T\n\nExample 9. The biconditional is the conjunction of its two implications.\n\n* P* Q* P ↔ Q* P → Q* Q → P* (P → Q) ∧ (Q → P)\n* T* T* T* T* T* T\n* T* F* F* F* T* F\n* F* T* F* T* F* F\n* F* F* T* T* T* T\n\nExample 10. The negation of an implication.\n\n* P* Q* P → Q* ¬(P → Q)* ¬Q* P ∧ ¬Q\n* T* T* T* F* F* F\n* T* F* F* T* T* T\n* F* T* T* F* F* F\n* F* F* T* F* T* F\n\nTruth tables decide any propositional question, but their size grows exponentially in the number of variables, and they do not extend to the quantifiers of Lecture 2. Deduction rules, applied one step at a time, scale and generalize. The next section presents them, and the rest of the lecture develops the corresponding proofs in Lean.\n\n","context":"Formal Software Verification\u0009Lecture 1: Motivation and Propositional Logic\u0009Logical Equivalence","header":"1.4.1. Examples","id":"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Logical-Equivalence/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Logical-Equivalence--Examples"}});